title: 13. Memory, ownership and regions
nav: Learn
order: 1300
template: docs
---
<p>Bayan has no garbage collector and no hidden allocation. You decide where memory comes from, and the compiler checks who owns each piece and how long a borrow of it can live. This chapter builds the mental model in four steps: where values live, what a copy and a move are, what a borrow is, and how an arena frees many things at once. Each step has a small program and the errors you will meet when you break its rules.</p>
<p>You need structs, lists and spans from the earlier chapters. The <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a> guide is the short reference for the same rules.</p>

<h2 id="where">Where values live</h2>
<p>Every value in a running program lives in one of four places.</p>
<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>Place</th><th>What lives there</th><th>When it is freed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Static</td><td>String literals and <code>const</code> data</td><td>Never. They last as long as the program.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stack</td><td>Locals, struct values, fixed-size arrays, <code>stackalloc</code> buffers</td><td>At the end of the scope that made them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heap</td><td>The storage behind a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> or <code>StrBuf</code>, taken from an allocator as it grows</td><td>When its single owner goes away.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arena</td><td>Everything allocated while an <code>Arena</code> is the current allocator</td><td>When the arena ends or is reset, all at once.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>Here is a picture of one running <code>main</code>. The boxes on the left are the variables in its stack frame. The arrows are the places where a variable only <em>points</em> at storage somewhere else.</p>
